A box that establishes a formatting context overlaps no float beside it.
Ladybird places such a box beside the floats at its top edge, and lays
it out. If its border box then overlaps a float, it places the box again
beside all the floats that its block size reaches, which narrows the box
or moves it down, and lays it out again, until it overlaps none.

Blink’s BlockLayoutAlgorithm::LayoutNewFormattingContext() goes through
the layout opportunities that ExclusionSpace::AllLayoutOpportunities()
finds, and lays the box out in each one until its fragment fits.
Gecko’s nsBlockFrame::ReflowBlockFrame() reflows the frame, asks
BlockReflowState::GetFloatAvailableSpaceForBSize() for the space over
the block size it got, and reflows it again where that space shrank,
moving on to the next band where FloatAvoidingBlockFitsInAvailSpace()
fails. WebKit’s RenderBox::shrinkLogicalWidthToAvoidFloats() measures
the floats over the block size that the box had at its last layout,
and RenderBlockFlow::layoutBlockChild() lays the box out again
where its position changed.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0 and WebKit 26.5, the test’s 23
cases come out the same. Where narrowing the box makes it reach a third,
wider float, Chrome and Firefox narrow it again, as Ladybird does, and
WebKit leaves it overlapping that float.
